course: dictionary
lesson: 9619
title: Struggle: more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one you need. «The pupil struggled with fractions, so she asked her teacher for help.» Here struggle means to try very hard to do something difficult.
explain: «The puppy struggled to get free from the tangled rope.» This struggle means to twist and fight to get loose. The word free is your clue: the puppy is trying to get out of something.
explain: A struggle can also be a noun, meaning a hard effort or a fight. «The climb up the steep hill was a struggle.» To check, put your meaning into the sentence: "The climb up the steep hill was a hard effort." It still makes sense, so you have it.
fact: To struggle means to try very hard to do something difficult.
fact: To struggle can also mean to fight or twist your body to get free.
fact: A struggle, as a noun, is a hard effort or a fight.
fact: Useful phrases: struggle with, struggle to, struggle free, a struggle for, it was a struggle.
fact: If someone struggles in water, do not jump in: shout for help and throw something that floats.
exercise: «The pupils struggled with the spelling test, but they tried again.» What does struggled mean here? | Laughed loudly at the answers | Fell asleep in class | *Tried very hard to do something difficult | Swam quickly across the pool
exercise: «The fox struggled to get free from the trap.» What does struggled mean here? | Slept under a tree | Ran away fast | *Twisted and fought to get loose | Called for help
exercise: «The climb up the mountain was a struggle.» In this sentence, what is a struggle? | *A hard effort | A map | A happy song | A cool drink
test: «The horse struggled on the slippery road.» What does struggled mean here? | Trotted happily past the shops | Ate its dinner in a field | Stopped to sleep | *Had a very hard time moving forward
test: Someone is struggling in deep water. What should you do first? | Jump in straight away to help them | *Shout for help and throw something that floats | Swim out without telling anyone | Wait and watch to see what happens
exam: Which sentence uses struggle as a noun? | «She will struggle up the hill after school.» | «They struggle with maths on Mondays.» | *«Reading the long book was a struggle for her.» | «The bird struggled to fly away.»
